WEBVTT

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:29.200
<v Ira Glass>Andrew Thomas Norway lives in St. Paul, but he's one of those doctors and nurses who flew to New York to help with flood of COVID patients who filled New York hospitals. He's actually at the hospital at Columbia University, where he went to medical school a quarter century ago. At night, after his 12-hour shifts, he sleeps in his old dormitory, literally in the dorm room he had when he was 23. The hospital has scratched out all kinds of extra space for COVID patients, converting 24 operating rooms into intensive care units. But the strangest thing to him?
<v Ira Glass>安德鲁·托马斯·挪威住在圣保罗，但他也是那些飞往纽约帮助应对涌入纽约各家医院的新冠患者的医生和护士之一。他现在就在哥伦比亚大学附属医院，25年前他曾在这里的医学院读书。每天晚上，在12小时的轮班结束后，他就睡在以前的老宿舍里，真的就睡在他23岁时住过的那个房间。医院为新冠患者腾出了各种额外空间，将24间手术室改造成了重症监护室。但对他来说，最奇怪的事情是什么呢？

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:47.710
<v Andrew Thomas>Normally, there'd be family all over the place. But the families are not able to come close to the hospital. They can't even come in the lobby, let alone get to see the people they love. And the other thing is just the reality that most of these people are going to die in these places alone.
<v Andrew Thomas>通常情况下，这里到处都会有家属。但家属们无法靠近医院。他们甚至不能进入大厅，更不用说去见他们所爱的人了。而另一件事是，这些人中的大多数注定要独自在这些地方死去。

00:00:47.710 --> 00:01:11.980
<v Ira Glass>Most of them are unconscious, in medically-induced comas, because it's easier to survive on a ventilator that way. But because they're unconscious, it means that the gap between these COVID patients and their families is even more profound, because they don't talk on the phone or FaceTime or anything. So Andrew suggested to the nurses that maybe they should take notes each day, little things they notice about each patient to share with the families if the patient dies. I asked him to read a few.
<v Ira Glass>他们中的大多数人都处于昏迷状态，是药物诱导的昏迷，因为那样在呼吸机上更容易存活。但由于他们处于昏迷状态，这意味着这些新冠患者与家人之间的隔阂更加深重，因为他们无法通电话或使用FaceTime等等。因此，安德鲁建议护士们或许应该每天记录下来，记录下他们注意到的关于每位患者的点滴，如果患者去世，可以与家属分享。我请他读了几条。

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:33.730
<v Andrew Thomas>Sure. This one's from a nurse named Sandy. And she wrote, Mrs-- I won't say the name. "The patient is mostly sleeping. She looks peaceful. Also, I really like her dark green fingernail polish. I would love to know the name of the color."
<v Andrew Thomas>当然。这一条是一位名叫桑迪的护士写的。她写道，女士——我不会说出名字。“这位病人大部分时间都在睡觉。她看起来很平静。另外，我真的很喜欢她深绿色的指甲油。我很想知道这个颜色的名字。”

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:50.810
<v Andrew Thomas>Here's another one. "I've been parting the patient's hair to the side. I don't know if that's how he wears it, but I think it looks good that way. Also, yesterday he got a shave, and now he looks so young."
<v Andrew Thomas>这是另一条。“我一直在把病人的头发分向一边。我不知道他平时是不是这样梳的，但我认为那样看起来不错。还有，昨天他刮了胡子，现在他看起来年轻多了。”

00:01:50.810 --> 00:02:03.750
<v Andrew Thomas>Here's one more. "The patient looks so peaceful. He's sleeping and calm. Everyone is jealous of his salt and pepper hair. He looks like George Clooney right now."
<v Andrew Thomas>这里还有一条。“这位病人看起来很平静。他正在睡觉，很安详。大家都嫉妒他那花白的头发。他现在看起来就像乔治·克鲁尼。”

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:15.190
<v Ira Glass>I talked to a few of the nurses who are writing these notes, and they said that, because the patients are unconscious, their interactions are pretty basic, so figuring out what to write can be tricky. Dr. Thomas sees that for sure.
<v Ira Glass>我和几位写这些笔记的护士谈过，她们说，因为病人们处于昏迷状态，他们的互动相当有限，所以要找出写什么内容可能会很棘手。托马斯医生当然也看到了这一点。

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:40.980
<v Andrew Thomas>Yeah, so people end up focusing on what you can see, which is embellishments like earrings and hairstyles and fingernail polish, and also just trying to emphasize that the people are comfortable, that they're sleeping. They're not in pain. That's something we really want to-- almost all the nurses comment on that, it seems, on almost every one.
<v Andrew Thomas>是的，所以人们最终会关注你能看到的东西，也就是耳环、发型和指甲油等修饰，同时也试图强调人们很舒适，他们正在睡觉。他们没有感到痛苦。这是我们真正想……似乎几乎所有的护士都会在每一条笔记中提到这一点。

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:45.000
<v Ira Glass>And what's your hope about how the families will take this?
<v Ira Glass>对于家属们会如何看待这一点，你有什么期望吗？

00:02:45.000 --> 00:03:04.520
<v Andrew Thomas>My hope is that the families will know that their loved ones were being seen as people and treated as people, and that they weren't alone as this happened.
<v Andrew Thomas>我的希望是，家属们会知道他们的亲人被当作人来看待，被当作人来关怀，而且在这一切发生时，他们并不孤单。

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:57.960
<v Ira Glass>So they gather details about the people laying in front of them, knowing that there's something like an 80% chance that, if they're on a ventilator with COVID, they won't live. Those are the numbers in New York. Those two things are so different in size, writing down a few tiny observations measured against the enormity of death and what that means. But sometimes, I think that's all you can do. And today on our program, we have two stories where ordinary people rise to this exact task. They look at death standing there-- gigantic, horrifying-- and they say something. And in these stories, truly amazing things happen. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>所以他们收集关于眼前躺着的人的细节，因为他们知道，如果他们因新冠使用呼吸机，他们活下来的几率大概只有80%。这些是纽约的数据。这两件事的量级如此悬殊，写下一些微小的观察结果来衡量死亡的巨大及其背后的意义。但有时，我想这就是你所能做的一切。今天在我们的节目中，我们有两个关于普通人挺身而出完成这一任务的故事。他们注视着死亡——那巨大、可怕的死亡——然后他们发出了声音。而在这些故事中，确实发生了令人惊叹的事情。来自芝加哥WBEZ广播电台，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:15.470
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Really Long Distance. So remember that tsunami that hit Japan? March 2011, giant black waves more than 30 feet tall hit Japan. A guy in a little town called Otsuchi shot this video. You can hear him yelling. All the houses are washing away.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，超长距离。还记得那场袭击日本的海啸吗？2011年3月，超过30英尺高的巨大黑色海浪袭击了日本。一个小镇叫大槌町，一个人拍摄了这段视频。你可以听到他在大喊大叫。所有的房子都被冲走了。

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:17.950
<v Man 1>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Man 1>[日语对话]

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:39.200
<v Ira Glass>Otsuchi had been there for 100 years, and in 30 minutes, it was gone, almost totally flattened. The tsunami and the earthquake that went with it killed six times more people than died in 9/11, over 19,000 people. Another 2,500 are still missing. In the aftermath, of course, families struggled to figure out how they were going to move forward without the people they loved.
<v Ira Glass>大槌町在那里已经有100年的历史了，而在30分钟内，它就消失了，几乎被完全夷为平地。海啸和随之而来的地震造成的死亡人数是“9·11”事件死亡人数的六倍，超过19000人。另有2500人至今下落不明。当然，事后，家庭都在努力寻找如何在没有所爱之人的情况下继续生活下去。

00:04:39.200 --> 00:05:04.580
<v Ira Glass>And in that town of Otsuchi, it led to this new-- I don't know, ritual is not exactly the right word for this, but it's something close to that-- this thing that people invented to stay connected to the dead. One of our producers, Miki Meek, has family in Japan, and she grew up going back and forth between there and here. And she watched this documentary about this thing people were doing in Otsuchi on the Japanese News Channel NHK, and got permission for us to play you some excerpts. Here's Miki.
<v Ira Glass>而在大槌町那个小镇，这导致了一种新的——我不知道，仪式这个词用在这里不太准确，但它很接近——人们发明了这种方式来与死者保持联系。我们的一位制片人米基·米克在日本有家人，她从小就在日本和这里之间往返。她在日本新闻频道NHK上看到了关于人们在大槌町所做之事的纪录片，并获得了授权，让我们播放其中的一些片段。接下来是米基。

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:31.670
<v Miki Meek>Of all the areas in Japan affected by the tsunami, Otsuchi has one of the highest numbers of missing people, 421. Today, it's still partly in ruins, and partly a construction site, as they try to rebuild the town on higher ground. But a year before the tsunami happened, this guy named Itaru Sasaki, he was already dealing with a loss. His cousin had just died, and Itaru was having a hard time figuring out how to talk about it.
<v Miki Meek>在日本所有受海啸影响的地区中，大槌町的失踪人数最多，为421人。今天，它仍然部分处于废墟之中，部分是建筑工地，因为他们试图在更高的地方重建小镇。但在海啸发生前一年，这个叫佐佐木格的人，已经在应对失去亲人的痛苦。他的堂弟刚刚去世，佐佐木正努力克服如何谈论这件事。

00:05:31.670 --> 00:06:07.220
<v Miki Meek>So he did something pretty ingenious. He went out and bought an old-fashioned phone booth and stuck it in his garden. It looks like an old English-style one. It's square and painted white, and has these glass window panes. Inside is a black rotary phone rusting on a wood shelf. This phone connected to nowhere. It didn't work at all. But that didn't matter to Itaru. He just needed a place where he felt like he could talk to his cousin, a place where he could air out his grief. And so putting an old phone booth in his garden, which sits on this little windy hill overlooking the Pacific Ocean, it felt like a perfect solution.
<v Miki Meek>所以他做了一件非常巧妙的事。他出去买了一个老式的电话亭，把它放在了花园里。它看起来像那种老式的英式电话亭。它是方形的，漆成白色，有玻璃窗格。里面是一部锈迹斑斑的黑色转盘电话，架在木架子上。这部电话连接着虚无。它根本无法使用。但这对佐佐木来说并不重要。他只是需要一个地方，让他觉得可以和堂弟交谈，一个可以发泄悲伤的地方。因此，把一个旧电话亭放在他花园里，坐落在俯瞰太平洋的小山坡上，这感觉像是一个完美的解决方案。

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:12.270
<v Itaru Sasaki>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Itaru Sasaki>[日语对话]

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:18.850
<v Miki Meek>He's saying, because my thoughts could not be relayed over a regular phone line, I wanted them to be carried on the wind.
<v Miki Meek>他说，因为我的思念无法通过普通的电话线路传达，我希望它们能被风带走。

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:21.350
<v Itaru Sasaki>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Itaru Sasaki>[日语对话]

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:53.260
<v Miki Meek>So I named it the Wind Telephone, [JAPANESE]. The idea of keeping up a relationship with the dead is not such a strange one in Japan. The line between our world and their world is thin. Lots of families keep a Buddhist altar for their dead relatives in the living room. My uncle has one for our family. There are photos on a little platform, and every day he leaves fresh fruit and rice for them, lights incense, and rings a bell. It's a way to stay in touch, to let them know that there's still a big part of our family.
<v Miki Meek>所以我把它命名为“风之电话”。在日本，与死者保持关系的想法并不奇怪。我们世界与他们世界之间的界限很薄。许多家庭在客厅里为死去的亲人供奉着佛教祭坛。我叔叔家里就有一个。在一个小平台上摆放着照片，他每天都会为他们供奉新鲜的水果和米饭，点上香，敲响铃铛。这是一种保持联系的方式，让他们知道他们仍然是我们家庭的重要组成部分。

00:06:53.260 --> 00:07:28.040
<v Miki Meek>So after the tsunami and earthquake happened, word got out about Itaru's special Wind Telephone, that he was using it as another way to stay connected to the dead. Soon, people started showing up randomly on his property and walking right into the phone booth. This has been going on for five years now. Itaru estimates that thousands of people from all over Japan have come to use his phone. A TV station asked Itaru and the people who come to use his phone if they could videotape their calls from a distance and put an audio recorder in the phone booth. They wanted to get a sense of how people are still grieving.
<v Miki Meek>所以海啸和地震发生后，关于佐佐木特别的“风之电话”的消息传开了，人们知道他正在用它作为与死者保持联系的另一种方式。很快，人们开始随机出现在他的土地上，直接走进电话亭。这种情况已经持续五年了。佐佐木估计，来自日本各地的数千人已经来使用过他的电话。一家电视台询问佐佐木和那些来用电话的人，是否可以从远处录制他们的通话，并在电话亭里放置一个录音机。他们想了解人们是如何在悲伤中坚持生活的。

00:07:28.040 --> 00:08:07.680
<v Miki Meek>I watched their documentary after the fifth anniversary of the tsunami back in March. That whole week, all the news programs in Japan were airing memorial programs. But I found the calls in this particular program remarkable and moving for just how simple they were. One woman from Otsuchi, named Sachiko Okawa, showed up one afternoon. She's 71 years old, and lost her husband in the tsunami. She regularly brings her two young grandsons to the phone booth, and you can tell by how casually they talked to him on the phone. They squish into the phone booth with their grandma, wearing matching blue and black striped shirts. Sachiko starts the call by picking up the receiver and saying hello.
<v Miki Meek>在三月份海啸五周年纪念日之后，我观看了他们的纪录片。那一整周，日本所有的电视节目都在播放纪念活动。但我发现这个特定节目中的通话非常引人注目和感人，仅仅是因为它们是如此简单。一位来自大槌町、名叫大川幸子的女士，一个下午出现在那里。她71岁，在海啸中失去了丈夫。她经常带着两个年幼的孙子来到电话亭，你可以从他们在大电话时随意的语气中看出。他们穿着蓝黑相间的条纹衫，和祖母一起挤进电话亭。大川幸子拿起听筒，说了声你好，开始了通话。

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:09.550
<v Sachiko Okawa>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Sachiko Okawa>[日语对话]

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:10.990
<v Miki Meek>Her oldest grandson quickly jumps in.
<v Miki Meek>她的大孙子很快插话。

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:12.250
<v Grandson 1>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Grandson 1>[日语对话]

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:12.940
<v Miki Meek>Hi, Grandpa.
<v Miki Meek>爷爷，你好。

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:13.900
<v Grandson 1>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Grandson 1>[日语对话]

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:14.870
<v Miki Meek>How are you?
<v Miki Meek>你好吗？

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:15.940
<v Grandson 1>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Grandson 1>[日语对话]

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:18.890
<v Miki Meek>I'll be in fourth grade next semester. Wasn't that fast?
<v Miki Meek>下学期我就要上四年级了。时间过得真快，不是吗？

00:08:18.890 --> 00:08:20.620
<v Grandson 1>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Grandson 1>[日语对话]

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:29.700
<v Miki Meek>Daina, my younger brother, he'll be in second grade next year. Then Sachiko corrects him. She says, no, Daina will be in second grade this year, not next.
<v Miki Meek>戴纳，我的弟弟，他明年上二年级。然后大川幸子纠正了他。她说，不对，戴纳今年上二年级，不是明年。

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:31.240
<v Grandson 1>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Grandson 1>[日语对话]

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:45.360
<v Miki Meek>Yeah, yeah, this year. A lot of calls were just like this, straightforward updates about life, the kind of quick highlights reel you might give to any family member you were catching up with on the phone. The boy, he then tells his grandpa--
<v Miki Meek>是的，是的，今年。很多通话都是这样的，关于生活的简单更新，就像你打电话给任何一个家庭成员聊近况时那种简短的要点回顾。男孩然后告诉他的爷爷——

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.360
<v Grandson 1>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Grandson 1>[日语对话]

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:49.180
<v Miki Meek>Grandma is fine, too.
<v Miki Meek>奶奶也很好。

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:50.440
<v Grandson 1>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Grandson 1>[日语对话]

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:56.440
<v Miki Meek>I'm giving the phone to Daina now. Daina, his little brother, grabs the phone.
<v Miki Meek>我现在把电话给戴纳。戴纳，他的小弟弟，抢过了电话。

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:59.200
<v Grandson 2>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Grandson 2>[日语对话]

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:04.060
<v Miki Meek>Grandpa, I finished all my homework. Sachiko urges him to keep talking.
<v Miki Meek>爷爷，我做完了所有的作业。大川幸子催促他继续说。

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:04.970
<v Sachiko Okawa>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Sachiko Okawa>[日语对话]

00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:07.060
<v Grandson 2>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Grandson 2>[日语对话]

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:13.310
<v Miki Meek>He says, everyone is doing fine. Then he hangs up. They all say goodbye.
<v Miki Meek>他说，大家都很好。然后他挂了电话。他们都说了再见。

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:14.630
<v Miki Meek>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Miki Meek>[交叉对话]

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:16.300
<v Grandson 1>Bye bye.
<v Grandson 1>再见。

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:18.340
<v Miki Meek>As they're walking out of the booth, Daina says--
<v Miki Meek>当他们走出电话亭时，戴纳说——

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:20.470
<v Grandson 2>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Grandson 2>[日语对话]

00:09:20.470 --> 00:09:29.830
<v Miki Meek>Maybe Grandpa will say he heard us.
<v Miki Meek>也许爷爷会说他听到了我们的话。

00:09:29.830 --> 00:10:09.760
<v Miki Meek>In another call, a woman in a puffy winter jacket with a fur-lined hood shows up at the booth by herself. Her name is Kikue Hirano, and she's 66. She used to live in Otsuchi, but she moved away after she lost her house and her husband in the tsunami. Her husband was a deep sea fisherman. His name was Miyoji and they used to talk and drink sake together at night. Now, Kikue lives alone, about 50 miles away. But sometimes Kikue finds herself driving back to Otsuchi, and to this booth. I watched her do this thing that a lot of callers seemed to do. You hear Kikue actually dialing the rotary phone, saying some numbers to herself.
<v Miki Meek>在另一次通话中，一位穿着毛领连帽羽绒服的女士独自来到电话亭。她的名字叫平野菊江，今年66岁。她曾经住在大槌町，但在海啸中失去家园和丈夫后搬走了。她的丈夫是一名深海渔民。他的名字叫美代治，他们过去常在晚上一起喝酒聊天。现在，菊江一个人住在50英里外的地方。但有时菊江会发现自己开车回到大槌町，回到这个电话亭。我看着她做着许多打电话的人似乎都会做的事情。你听到菊江实际上在拨动转盘电话，嘴里自言自语说着数字。

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:11.920
<v Kikue Hirano>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Kikue Hirano>[日语对话]

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:35.790
<v Miki Meek>4, 2, 5, 7, 4, 4. She's dialing the phone number for her old house in Otsuchi, the last place she knew to reach her husband. Then Kikue just stands in the booth in silence, holding the phone to her ear.
<v Miki Meek>4、2、5、7、4、4。她正在拨打她在旧大槌町家的号码，那是她最后知道能联系上她丈夫的地方。然后菊江只是静静地站在电话亭里，把电话贴在耳边。

00:10:35.790 --> 00:11:14.330
<v Miki Meek>Sometimes she fidgets around, and tilts her head up and concentrates on the ceiling, the same way I do when I want to cry but I'm trying hard not to. It doesn't work. Kikue brushes some tears off her face. Eventually, she hangs up. She lingers in the booth a little longer, hands clasped together in front of her, staring at the phone booth floor. She walks out.
<v Miki Meek>有时她会烦躁不安，抬头看向天花板，集中精神，这和我想要哭却努力忍住时的样子一样。但这没用。菊江擦掉脸上的泪水。最终，她挂断了电话。她在电话亭里又逗留了一会儿，双手合十在身前，盯着电话亭的地板。她走了出去。

00:11:14.330 --> 00:11:49.180
<v Miki Meek>One pattern that the owner of the phone booth, Itaru, has noticed over the years is that more men than women come to use it. Not surprisingly, this is not a demographic that's known for sharing their feelings, especially the older farmers. They already have a reputation for not talking much. In one of the phone calls recorded in winter, a man with gray hair and a little towel hanging around his neck walks into the garden. This kind of towel is part of the uniform of Japanese farmers. They use them to wipe away sweat and clean their hands. This man opens the telephone booth door, and under his breath, you hear him say--
<v Miki Meek>电话亭主人佐佐木多年来注意到的一个模式是，男性比女性更多地来使用它。不出所料，这并不是一个以分享感受著称的群体，尤其是老农民。他们已经以不爱说话而闻名。在冬季录下的一通电话中，一个脖子上围着小毛巾、头发花白的男人走进花园。这种毛巾是日本农民制服的一部分。他们用它擦汗和清洁双手。这个男人打开电话亭的门，你可以听到他压低声音说——

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:50.660
<v Man 2>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Man 2>[日语对话]

00:11:50.660 --> 00:12:20.640
<v Miki Meek>Huh, so this is the Wind Telephone. This appears to be his very first visit to the phone booth. He lost his oldest son in the tsunami. His son's name was Nobuyuki. He also lost his house and had to move into temporary housing with his wife. And recently, she got sick and also passed away. He calls her okaasan. It means mom in Japanese. It's what everyone in the family calls a female head of the house, even the husband. It's a very intimate, loving term.
<v Miki Meek>哼，这就是“风之电话”啊。这似乎是他第一次造访电话亭。他在海啸中失去了长子。他儿子的名字叫信之。他还失去了房子，不得不和妻子住进临时住房。最近，她生病去世了。他称呼她为“妈妈”。这是日语里的“妈妈”。这是家里每个人对女性家长的称呼，甚至连丈夫也这样叫。这是一个非常亲密、充满爱意的称呼。

