<v Man 3>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Man 3>[日语对话]

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:55.310
<v Miki Meek>Eat something, anything.
<v Miki Meek>吃点东西，什么都行。

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:56.930
<v Man 3>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Man 3>[日语对话]

00:14:56.930 --> 00:15:04.440
<v Miki Meek>Just be alive. Somewhere, anywhere.
<v Miki Meek>只要活着就好。在什么地方都行，在哪都行。

00:15:04.440 --> 00:15:05.420
<v Man 3>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Man 3>[日语对话]

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:19.790
<v Miki Meek>I'm so lonely.
<v Miki Meek>我好寂寞。

00:15:19.790 --> 00:15:45.960
<v Miki Meek>He never says I love you directly. Real feelings are communicated through small gestures, especially ones of concern, like when he asks his wife, are you staying warm? Are you eating? And then promising, I'll build a house for us. These are total heartfelt declarations of love.
<v Miki Meek>他从不直接说“我爱你”。真实的感受是通过微小的举动传达的，特别是那些关心的细节，比如当他问妻子：你冷吗？你吃了吗？然后承诺说：我会为我们建一栋房子。这些都是发自内心的深爱宣言。

00:15:45.960 --> 00:16:21.540
<v Miki Meek>For other men, the phone booth is a place where they can finally say their complicated feelings out loud. They can voice their regrets. Anyone who's had someone close to them die knows this feeling. Like, I've kept having the same one-way conversation with my dad in my head ever since he passed away last year. I just keep telling him all the situations where I wish I had been kinder, more patient. One call I watched was from a young father with rectangle glasses and a long black jacket. He lost his family, both parents, his wife-- her name was Mine-- and a one-year-old son named Issei.
<v Miki Meek>对于其他男人来说，电话亭是一个他们终于可以大声说出复杂情绪的地方。他们可以吐露遗憾。任何有过亲近之人去世经历的人都知道这种感觉。就像，自从我父亲去年去世后，我脑海中一直进行着同样的单向对话。我只是不停地告诉他，我真希望在那些情况下自己能更友善、更耐心一些。我看到的其中一通通话来自一位戴着长方形眼镜、穿着黑色长外套的年轻父亲。他失去了全家，父母双亡，妻子——她的名字叫美乃——还有一个一岁的儿子叫一诚。

