<v Ren's Sister>[SOBS]
<v Ren's Sister>[哭泣声]

00:23:24.040 --> 00:23:27.910
<v Miki Meek>She says, are you trying to make me laugh, Riku? Riku says--
<v Miki Meek>她说，你是想逗我笑吗，陆？陆说——

00:23:27.910 --> 00:23:29.610
<v Riku>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Riku>[日语对话]

00:23:29.610 --> 00:23:38.950
<v Miki Meek>--no, I'm not. Then she finally starts talking to her dad. The conversation is all over the place. And she's crying so hard she can barely talk.
<v Miki Meek>——没有，我没有。然后她终于开始对父亲说话。谈话很杂乱。她哭得太厉害，几乎无法开口。

00:23:38.950 --> 00:23:40.210
<v Ren's Sister>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Ren's Sister>[日语对话]

00:23:40.210 --> 00:23:59.280
<v Miki Meek>Dad, I'm so sorry I always used to say you were stinky. What happened to your promise to buy me a violin? Now I'll have to buy one myself.
<v Miki Meek>爸爸，对不起，以前我总说你臭。你答应给我买的小提琴呢？现在我得自己买了。

00:23:59.280 --> 00:24:06.240
<v Miki Meek>Her little brother who sticks close to her, he encourages her to keep going. He says, what else? So she tells her dad--
<v Miki Meek>一直紧贴着她的小弟弟鼓励她继续说。他说，还有呢？所以她告诉父亲——

00:24:06.240 --> 00:24:09.820
<v Ren's Sister>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Ren's Sister>[日语对话]

00:24:09.820 --> 00:24:24.510
<v Miki Meek>I started tennis in junior high school. I'm not in the top eight. I want to be in the top eight in our last tournament. Please cheer for me.
<v Miki Meek>我在初中开始打网球了。我没进前八名。我希望能在我们最后一场比赛中进前八。请为我加油。

00:24:24.510 --> 00:24:32.850
<v Miki Meek>I got hooked on this boy band, The Johnnys, when I was in my first year of junior high. I'm still hooked. They both say--
<v Miki Meek>我在读初一的时候迷上了一个男孩乐队“The Johnnys”。我现在还迷着呢。他们俩都说——

00:24:32.850 --> 00:24:34.440
<v Ren's Sister>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Ren's Sister>[日语对话]

00:24:34.440 --> 00:24:41.820
<v Miki Meek>--goodbye. They come out of the booth together. Now it's their mom's turn. Her kids tell her itterasshai.
<v Miki Meek>——再见。他们一起走出了电话亭。现在轮到妈妈了。孩子们对她说“一路走好”。

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:42.870
<v Ren Kozaki>Itterasshai.
<v Ren Kozaki>一路走好。

00:24:42.870 --> 00:24:53.820
<v Miki Meek>Have a good trip. Good luck. Ren, the oldest, he bows and waves to her. She walks in, picks up the phone, and lets out a big sigh.
<v Miki Meek>旅途平安。祝你好运。莲，作为老大，他鞠躬向她挥手。她走进去，拿起电话，深深地叹了一口气。

00:24:53.820 --> 00:25:04.240
<v Mom>[SIGHS]
<v Mom>[叹气声]

00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:06.460
<v Mom>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Mom>[日语对话]

00:25:06.460 --> 00:25:10.570
<v Miki Meek>Where should I start?
<v Miki Meek>我该从哪儿开始呢？

00:25:10.570 --> 00:25:13.370
<v Mom>[SIGHS]
<v Mom>[叹气声]

00:25:13.370 --> 00:25:16.060
<v Mom>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Mom>[日语对话]

00:25:16.060 --> 00:25:23.500
<v Miki Meek>I feel like you're still alive somewhere. We had so many things we wanted to do together.
<v Miki Meek>我觉得你还在某个地方活着。我们还有好多事想一起做。

00:25:23.500 --> 00:25:33.300
<v Mom>[SNIFFS]
<v Mom>[吸鼻涕声]

00:25:33.300 --> 00:25:35.320
<v Mom>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Mom>[日语对话]

00:25:35.320 --> 00:25:57.250
<v Miki Meek>Over the phone we always said to each other, are you alive? Yes, I'm alive. It was our password between the two of us, wasn't it? I can't ask you that anymore.
<v Miki Meek>以前打电话时我们总是对彼此说，你还活着吗？是的，我活着。那是我们之间的暗号，对吧？我不能再那样问你了。

00:25:57.250 --> 00:25:58.800
<v Mom>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Mom>[日语对话]

00:25:58.800 --> 00:26:05.340
<v Miki Meek>Come back. We, all four of us together, we will be waiting. Bye.
<v Miki Meek>回来吧。我们四个，都会等你。再见。

00:26:05.340 --> 00:26:08.670
<v Miki Meek>[PHONE HANGING UP]
<v Miki Meek>[电话挂断声]

00:26:08.670 --> 00:26:12.040
<v Miki Meek>After she comes out, the family lingers outside the phone booth for a while.
<v Miki Meek>她出来后，全家在电话亭外徘徊了一会儿。

00:26:12.040 --> 00:26:15.060
<v Miki Meek>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Miki Meek>[日语对话]

00:26:15.060 --> 00:26:31.260
<v Miki Meek>This was literally the first time they'd all talked about their dad together since the tsunami happened five years ago. The youngest, Riku, he sat quietly on a bench with his head in his hands. The night before his dad disappeared, they went to a public bath together. His mom and older siblings tell him--
<v Miki Meek>这实际上是自海啸发生五年来，他们第一次一起谈论父亲。最小的陆，安静地坐在长椅上，双手抱头。父亲失踪的前一天晚上，他们一起去了公共浴室。妈妈和年长的孩子对他说——

00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:33.540
<v Riku>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Riku>[日语对话]

00:26:33.540 --> 00:26:42.700
<v Miki Meek>--Riku, you don't need to keep your feelings in. Go ahead and cry when you want to. Ren, the oldest, gently teases him. He says--
<v Miki Meek>——陆，不需要把感情憋在心里。想哭的时候就哭吧。莲，作为老大，温柔地打趣他。他说——

00:26:42.700 --> 00:26:43.380
<v Ren Kozaki>[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
<v Ren Kozaki>[日语对话]

00:26:43.380 --> 00:27:02.270
<v Miki Meek>--see, he can't stop crying. Mom says yeah, but he held back until now. It's OK because you held back. You endured until now.
<v Miki Meek>——看，他哭个不停。妈妈说，是啊，但他一直忍到现在。没关系，因为你忍住了。你坚持到现在。

00:27:02.270 --> 00:27:26.430
<v Miki Meek>The sister then hands Riku her handkerchief. The mom says to her kids, we were all about to fall apart. We were so broken. We didn't think we could make it through. And maybe that's why we never talked about Dad until now. But talking to him on the phone today, it changed something.
<v Miki Meek>妹妹然后把手帕递给陆。妈妈对孩子们说，我们当时都快崩溃了。我们伤透了心。我们以为我们挺不过去了。也许这就是为什么我们直到今天才提起爸爸。但今天在电话里和他交谈，改变了一些东西。

00:27:26.430 --> 00:27:44.970
<v Ira Glass>Miki Meek is one of the producers of our program. Thanks to Tomohiko Yokoyama and NHK Sendai, who recorded all this and shared it with us. Today's show is mostly a rerun. We checked this week, and the wind phone is still in operation, though not many visitors right now because of COVID. The Japanese government is asking people to stay home.
<v Ira Glass>米基·米克是我们节目的制片人之一。感谢横山智彦和NHK仙台，他们录下了这一切并与我们分享。今天的节目大多是重播。我们这周查过，风之电话还在运营中，虽然因为新冠疫情现在来访者不多。日本政府正在呼吁人们居家。

00:27:44.970 --> 00:27:58.550
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, two brothers in their 80s have a last chance to talk to each other before they die, and neither wants to take it. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，一对80多岁的兄弟在去世前有最后一次交流的机会，但谁都不想抓住它。芝加哥公共广播将在节目继续时带来这部分内容。

00:27:58.550 --> 00:28:09.190
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, One Last Thing Before I Go, stories that are about-- well, they're about what power do words have in the face of death?
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目主题是“我离开前的最后一件事”，讲述的是——嗯，是关于在死亡面前语言拥有什么力量？

00:28:09.190 --> 00:28:13.970
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at Act Two of our program-- Act Two, Uncle's Keeper.
<v Ira Glass>我们已经到了节目的第二幕——第二幕，叔叔的守护者。

00:28:13.970 --> 00:28:39.290
<v Ira Glass>So in the first act of our program, people kind of defy death by making small talk and some talk that was not so small with their dead relatives. In this act, somebody wants to do something very similar, make a connection that seemed impossible between people. Except in this story, all the relatives are alive, which you'd think would make it super easy. The story comes from Jonathan Goldstein.
<v Ira Glass>在节目的第一幕中，人们通过与已故亲人的闲聊，甚至是一些意义重大的交谈，挑战了死亡。在这一幕中，有人想要做非常相似的事情，在人与人之间建立看似不可能的联系。除了在这个故事里，所有的亲戚都还活着，你可能会认为这会让事情变得超级简单。这个故事来自乔纳森·戈尔茨坦。

00:28:39.290 --> 00:28:39.920
<v Buzz Goldstein>Hello?
<v Buzz Goldstein>喂？

00:28:39.920 --> 00:28:40.910
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Hey, Dad?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>嘿，爸？

00:28:40.910 --> 00:28:41.690
<v Buzz Goldstein>Hi, Johnny.
<v Buzz Goldstein>嘿，乔尼。

00:28:41.690 --> 00:28:42.920
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Hey. How you doing?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>嘿。你好吗？

00:28:42.920 --> 00:28:43.920
<v Buzz Goldstein>Good, you?
<v Buzz Goldstein>挺好，你呢？

00:28:43.920 --> 00:28:46.220
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Good, good. Good Yuntif.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>挺好，挺好。祝节日快乐。

00:28:46.220 --> 00:28:47.370
<v Buzz Goldstein>Shana Tova.
<v Buzz Goldstein>新年快乐。

00:28:47.370 --> 00:28:48.610
<v Jonathan Goldstein>[HEBREW]
<v Jonathan Goldstein>[希伯来语]

00:28:48.610 --> 00:28:50.940
<v Buzz Goldstein>[HEBREW]. [CHUCKLES] What's that mean?
<v Buzz Goldstein>[希伯来语]。[轻笑]那是什么意思？

00:28:50.940 --> 00:28:51.440
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I'm not sure.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我不确定。

00:28:51.440 --> 00:28:52.820
<v Buzz Goldstein>Oh, oh.
<v Buzz Goldstein>哦，哦。

00:28:52.820 --> 00:29:07.970
<v Jonathan Goldstein>This is my father, Buzz. I'm calling him at his home in Montreal. And the reason we're talking crazy talk is because it's Yom Kippur, the Jewish day of atonement, which seems as good a day as any to talk with him about forgiveness.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>这是我的父亲，巴兹。我正在打给他在蒙特利尔的家。我们说些乱七八糟的话是因为今天是赎罪日，犹太人的忏悔日，这似乎是和他讨论宽恕的好日子。

00:29:07.970 --> 00:29:14.220
<v Jonathan Goldstein>So I wanted to-- I wanted to ask you something, and I just wanted to gauge your interest.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>所以我想要——我想要问你一件事，我只是想试探一下你的兴趣。

00:29:14.220 --> 00:29:16.460
<v Buzz Goldstein>Yeah.
<v Buzz Goldstein>嗯。

00:29:16.460 --> 00:29:21.740
<v Jonathan Goldstein>How would you feel about paying your brother, Sheldon, a visit?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你觉得去探望一下你弟弟谢尔顿怎么样？

00:29:21.740 --> 00:29:25.340
<v Buzz Goldstein>I have no feelings about it. I'm not really interested.
<v Buzz Goldstein>我对此没什么感觉。我不怎么感兴趣。

00:29:25.340 --> 00:29:26.870
<v Jonathan Goldstein>You're not?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>不感兴趣？

00:29:26.870 --> 00:29:29.030
<v Buzz Goldstein>No.
<v Buzz Goldstein>不。

00:29:29.030 --> 00:29:55.460
<v Jonathan Goldstein>My father, Buzz, is 80, and his brother, Sheldon, his only sibling, is 85. And for the past 40 years, they've pretty much been on the outs. My father lives in Montreal, and Sheldon lives in Florida. And the last time they saw each other, over 20 years ago, was at their mother's funeral when they had a fight over the details of the arrangement. Since then, they've hardly spoken.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我的父亲巴兹今年80岁，他的弟弟谢尔顿，也就是他唯一的兄弟，今年85岁。过去40年里，他们几乎一直处于断联状态。我父亲住在蒙特利尔，谢尔顿住在佛罗里达。他们最后一次见面是在20多年前母亲的葬礼上，当时他们因为安排细节发生了争吵。从那以后，他们几乎没怎么说过话。

00:29:55.460 --> 00:30:13.520
<v Jonathan Goldstein>It worries me because there's not a lot of time left, and I don't want my father to have regrets. And my father has a profound capacity for regret. My mother gave up trying to reunite them years ago after many attempts. So I know that if I don't push him, no one else will.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>这让我担心，因为没剩多少时间了，我不希望父亲留下遗憾。而我的父亲有着强烈的遗憾倾向。我母亲多年来经过多次尝试后放弃了撮合他们。所以我知道，如果我不推动他，就没有别人会做了。

00:30:13.520 --> 00:30:16.070
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I'm not surprised that you're not jumping at the idea, but I'm a little surprised--
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我不惊讶你没有对这个想法表现出极大的热情，但我有一点点惊讶——

00:30:16.070 --> 00:30:16.310
<v Buzz Goldstein>No.
<v Buzz Goldstein>不。

00:30:16.310 --> 00:30:19.790
<v Jonathan Goldstein>--that you're as against the idea.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>——你会如此反对这个想法。

00:30:19.790 --> 00:30:26.180
<v Buzz Goldstein>Yeah. Time's passed. He hasn't shown much interest. So I'm respecting that, and I leave him alone.
<v Buzz Goldstein>嗯。时间过了很久。他没表现出太大的兴趣。所以我尊重这一点，我就不再打扰他了。

00:30:26.180 --> 00:30:33.800
<v Jonathan Goldstein>What he did do was he called you on your 80th birthday not so long ago, and you felt good about that.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>他确实在不久前你的80岁生日时打过电话给你，你感觉挺好。

00:30:33.800 --> 00:30:36.320
<v Buzz Goldstein>Yeah, because I called him on his 80th birthday.
<v Buzz Goldstein>嗯，因为我在他80岁生日时打给了他。

00:30:36.320 --> 00:30:40.800
<v Jonathan Goldstein>This kind of tit for tat accounting is what always gets in the way.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>这种斤斤计较的态度总是横亘在中间。

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:52.570
<v Buzz Goldstein>You know what it is at this point with him? I'll tell you what it is. I don't think it's even anger. He's past anger. And he's past any feelings of animosity. He's pissed. He just doesn't care.
<v Buzz Goldstein>你知道他在这一点上是怎么想的吗？我告诉你吧。我想那甚至不是愤怒。他已经过了愤怒的阶段。他已经没有了任何敌意。他是厌烦了。他只是不在乎。

00:30:52.570 --> 00:30:53.970
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Yeah.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>嗯。

00:30:53.970 --> 00:31:01.530
<v Buzz Goldstein>That's apathy. I mean, sometimes at least hate or love, they're emotions. Apathy is nothing.
<v Buzz Goldstein>那是冷漠。我是说，有时候至少恨或爱，它们都是情感。冷漠意味着什么都没有。

00:31:01.530 --> 00:31:02.520
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Yeah.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>嗯。

00:31:02.520 --> 00:31:15.200
<v Buzz Goldstein>You know what, Johnny? As a child, even when I was 10, when I was nine and eight, I was crazy about him. We had a great-- I loved him. He was the older brother. He would-- hello?
<v Buzz Goldstein>你知道吗，乔尼？小时候，哪怕在我10岁，9岁和8岁的时候，我都很迷他。我们有一段很棒的——我爱他。他是哥哥。他会——喂？

00:31:15.200 --> 00:31:18.870
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Yeah, yeah, yeah. I'm listening.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>在，在，在。我在听。

00:31:18.870 --> 00:31:29.820
<v Buzz Goldstein>I just looked up to him. And he had all the friends. Sometimes he'd take me along with him. And he was [INAUDIBLE]. Somebody tried to call here, dinging me here.
<v Buzz Goldstein>我刚才崇拜他。他有很多朋友。有时候他会带上我。他当时是[听不清]。有人想打进来，在这响个不停。

00:31:29.820 --> 00:32:06.370
<v Jonathan Goldstein>The most complicated question, the one I keep coming back to, is how did the bad blood begin? And there are many versions-- an ill-fated trip to Montreal where Sheldon felt slighted about having to stay in my father's basement, an ill-fated trip to New York where my father felt slighted about having to stay in Sheldon's attic, rude words spoken to each other's wives. In one version of the story, Sheldon's refusal to bring a table to my bris almost resulted in my being circumcised on an ironing board. But in the version being told today, my father was asked by Sheldon to pay more than his fair share for their mother's funeral.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>最复杂的问题，也是我不断回来的问题，是这些积怨是怎么开始的？有很多版本——一次去蒙特利尔的不幸旅行，谢尔顿觉得必须住在父亲地下室里受到了冷落；一次去纽约的不幸旅行，父亲觉得必须住在谢尔顿阁楼里受到了冷落；对彼此的妻子说了粗鲁的话。在一个版本的故事里，谢尔顿拒绝为我的割礼仪式准备一张桌子，差点导致我只能在熨衣板上进行手术。但在今天讲述的版本里，父亲被谢尔顿要求支付超过他份额的母亲葬礼费用。

00:32:06.370 --> 00:32:18.240
<v Buzz Goldstein>And I said, you're always working some kind of an angle. So then he got furious. He got furious. He started screaming at the phone, go to hell, drop dead, blah, blah, blah.
<v Buzz Goldstein>我说，你总是带着某种目的。于是他火冒三丈。他火冒三丈。他在电话那头尖叫着，下地狱去吧，去死吧，等等等等。

00:32:18.240 --> 00:32:30.420
<v Buzz Goldstein>That was how that ended. But I feel he's the kind of guy-- he has angles like that. He has angles. I always felt I was on the up and up with him, and he wasn't with me.
<v Buzz Goldstein>这就是那样结束的。但我感觉他就是那种人——他总带着那样的算计。他总是有算计。我一直觉得自己对他诚心诚意，而他却不是。

00:32:30.420 --> 00:32:36.810
<v Jonathan Goldstein>If you got a stronger sense that he was interested in seeing you, then would you--
<v Jonathan Goldstein>如果你能有更强的感觉他想见你，那么你会——

00:32:36.810 --> 00:32:37.860
<v Buzz Goldstein>Yes, yes.
<v Buzz Goldstein>会，会。

00:32:37.860 --> 00:32:39.320
<v Jonathan Goldstein>--you would be more inclined to see him?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>——你会更有兴趣见他？

00:32:39.320 --> 00:32:42.000
<v Buzz Goldstein>I wouldn't stay at his house though. That's out of the question.
<v Buzz Goldstein>不过我不会住在他家里。那是不可能的。

00:32:42.000 --> 00:33:00.270
<v Jonathan Goldstein>OK, quick sidebar-- anytime I've ever raised the prospect of visiting Sheldon, no matter how hypothetical the scenario, my father always makes a point of insisting how no matter what he would not stay in Sheldon's house, even if he was invited to, which, I should point out, he never is.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>OK，快速侧记一下——不管我什么时候提起探望谢尔顿的可能性，无论场景多么假设性，我父亲总是强调无论如何他都不会住进谢尔顿家里，即使他受邀——我应该指出，他从来没有受到邀请。

00:33:00.270 --> 00:33:01.980
<v Buzz Goldstein>I wouldn't stay at his house.
<v Buzz Goldstein>我不会住在他家。

00:33:01.980 --> 00:33:03.400
<v Jonathan Goldstein>How come you-- [LAUGHS]
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你怎么总——[笑]

00:33:03.400 --> 00:33:06.780
<v Buzz Goldstein>I wouldn't stay there. I mean, that's not my thing.
<v Buzz Goldstein>我不会住在那里。我是说，那不是我的风格。

00:33:06.780 --> 00:33:09.030
<v Jonathan Goldstein>How come you always bring that up? I mean, normally--
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你怎么总提这事？我是说，通常——

00:33:09.030 --> 00:33:09.970
<v Buzz Goldstein>Of course. I don't feel comfortable.
<v Buzz Goldstein>当然。我不觉得舒服。

00:33:09.970 --> 00:33:15.330
<v Jonathan Goldstein>--when someone goes to visit someone that they haven't seen in decades, they'll stay at a hotel.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>——当一个人去探望几十年没见的人时，他们会住在旅馆。

00:33:15.330 --> 00:33:17.850
<v Buzz Goldstein>I would stay at a motel or somewhere near his place.
<v Buzz Goldstein>我会住在汽车旅馆或是他附近的地方。

00:33:17.850 --> 00:33:22.890
<v Jonathan Goldstein>A motel. Yeah. No, we'd get a place with an ice machine and, you know.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>汽车旅馆。好的。我们会找个带制冰机的，你知道的。

00:33:22.890 --> 00:33:26.970
<v Buzz Goldstein>Why? You're interested in making a trip?
<v Buzz Goldstein>为什么？你有兴趣去旅行？

00:33:26.970 --> 00:33:37.370
<v Jonathan Goldstein>[SIGHS] I mean, I'm interested-- do you think that there's anything to be gained in seeing him?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>[叹气]我是说，我有兴趣——你觉得见他会有什么收获吗？

00:33:37.370 --> 00:33:50.610
<v Buzz Goldstein>Hm. I guess there's something. You know, you share your common experience, and you talk about the old days, and there are things that only he and I can remember.
<v Buzz Goldstein>嗯。我想会有一些。你知道，你们分享共同的经历，谈论过去的日子，有些东西只有我和他能记得。

00:33:50.610 --> 00:33:53.340
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Yeah.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>嗯。

00:33:53.340 --> 00:34:04.890
<v Buzz Goldstein>What you could do is you could call him and see what his attitude is. It depends on how you feel, what kind of reception you get.
<v Buzz Goldstein>你可以做的是打给他，看看他的态度。取决于你的感觉，取决于你得到什么样的回应。

00:34:04.890 --> 00:34:08.650
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Yeah. I mean, I would. I would be happy to do that. My concern is that--
<v Jonathan Goldstein>嗯。我是说，我会的。我很高兴这样做。我的担忧是——

00:34:08.650 --> 00:34:14.430
<v Buzz Goldstein>I like your initial suggestion that you call and feel him out, see what he's like.
<v Buzz Goldstein>我喜欢你最初的建议，打给他试探一下，看他是什么态度。

00:34:14.430 --> 00:34:18.900
<v Jonathan Goldstein>OK, I didn't suggest that, but-- you suggested that.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>OK，我没那样建议，但——是你建议的。

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:23.670
<v Buzz Goldstein>Yeah, I like that. But you'll give me an honest reaction.
<v Buzz Goldstein>嗯，我喜欢那样。但你会给我一个真实的反应。

00:34:23.670 --> 00:34:31.600
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I'm happy to do it, but-- I mean, what are you looking for from-- what do you want to hear from him?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我很乐意做，但——我是说，你从——你想从他那里听到什么？

00:34:31.600 --> 00:34:33.650
<v Buzz Goldstein>I miss my brother. I would like to see him.
<v Buzz Goldstein>我想念我的弟弟。我想见见他。

00:34:33.650 --> 00:34:34.950
<v Jonathan Goldstein>OK.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>OK。

